has a temperature of 38.1°C and an erythematous pharynx with no exudate. She is otherwise','{"A":"urine dipstick is positive for blood for which you order a urinalysis (UA). UA is reported\nback significant for red blood cells, 1+ proteinuria, no WBCs, and negative for nitrite. Which one\nof the following would be the next best step in management?\n\n¥ OA. Repeat UA after the URTI resolves.","B":"Urine culture.","C":"24-hour urine exam.","D":"Ultrasonography.","E":"Start her on corticosteroids."}','A','Asymptomatic hematuria in children, in the setting of a febrile illness other than urinary tract
infection, should be assessed after the febrile illness subsides; provided that the illness is not
due to urinary tract infection (UTI). With UTI, the next step would be a urine culture. This patient
neither has urinary symptoms, nor laboratory findings consistent with UTI; therefore, no urine
culture is indicated.

Asymptomatic microscopic hematuria should always be confirmed with 2 positive urine exams
out of 3 in 2-3 weeks before any further assessment is considered. However, prompt action is
mandated in the presence of symptoms such as hypertension, edema, or renal failure.','{"source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Pediatric amedex 2025.pdf","supporting_pages":[],"source_qid":null,"duplicate_group_id":"exact-5ec05ca88ac3","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"b660b52f4f80a1da3f81","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"Pediatric amedex 2025.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Pediatric amedex 2025.pdf","source_page":7,"source_qid":null}],"parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"strict_ocr_tolerant","text_method":"tesseract_retry_200dpi_psm3","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'needs_verification','[]','medium','{"record_id":"b660b52f4f80a1da3f81","source_file":"Pediatric amedex 2025.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Pediatric amedex 2025.pdf","source_year":2025,"source_page":7,"source_qid":null,"domain_hint":"Paediatrics","stem":"A 15-year-old girl presents with an upper respiratory tract infection (URTI). On examination, she\nhas a temperature of 38.1°C and an erythematous pharynx with no exudate. She is otherwise","options":{"A":"urine dipstick is positive for blood for which you order a urinalysis (UA). UA is reported\nback significant for red blood cells, 1+ proteinuria, no WBCs, and negative for nitrite. Which one\nof the following would be the next best step in management?\n\n¥ OA. Repeat UA after the URTI resolves.","B":"Urine culture.","C":"24-hour urine exam.","D":"Ultrasonography.","E":"Start her on corticosteroids."},"source_answer":"A","source_explanation":"Asymptomatic hematuria in children, in the setting of a febrile illness other than urinary tract\ninfection, should be assessed after the febrile illness subsides; provided that the illness is not\ndue to urinary tract infection (UTI). With UTI, the next step would be a urine culture. This patient\nneither has urinary symptoms, nor laboratory findings 