fluid intake, toileting, or an appropriate reward system. Enuresis alarms work best for well-motivated families and children with frequent
enuresis (more than twice per week). Other treatment options should be used if:

¢ Rapid or short-term improvement is the goal

¢ The child or parents do not want to try the enuresis alarm

¢ The child wets the bed only once or twice per week

e The parents are having emotional difficulty coping with the burden of bedwetting
e The parents are expressing anger, negativity, or blame toward the child

Bedwetting of this 4-year-old child is normal and does not require investigation or treatment for now.

(Option B) Desmopressin, a synthetic vasopressin analog, is the first-line treatment for enuresis in children older than 5 years, whose
bedwetting has not responded to advice about fluid intake, toileting, or an appropriate reward system. It is an alternative to enuresis alarms for
children and families who seek rapid or short-term improvement of enuresis or have failed, refused, or are unlikely to adhere to enuresis alarm
treatment and for whom an enuresis alarm is unsuitable.

(Option C) Tricyclic antidepressants (TCAs) decrease the amount of time spent in REM sleep, stimulate vasopressin secretion, and relax the
detrusor muscle. Given the efficacy and safety of enuresis alarms and desmopressin, tricyclic antidepressants (e.g., amitriptyline, desipramine,
imipramine) are used as a third-line treatment for monosymptomatic enuresis after failed alarm therapy and/or desmopressin).

(Option E) Ultrasonography of the urinary system and urine examination are initial investigations to consider in children with enuresis. This
child is only 4 years old and does not fulfill the criteria for enuresis; therefore, no evaluation is indicated for him at this stage.',NULL,'Retain the source-era answer for question-bank study; verify current Australian guidance before clinical use.',NULL,NULL,'2025','v2.9.4',1,'text','{"source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Pediatric amedex 2025.pdf","supporting_pages":[35],"source_qid":null,"duplicate_group_id":"exact-f070aeafeeea","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"2332d43ae844c1bd92f1","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"Pediatric amedex 2025.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Pediatric amedex 2025.pdf","source_page":35,"source_qid":null},{"record_id":"fdaea6d23592046c7f57","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"AMEDEX 2025 PAEDIATRIC (2).pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX 2025.09更新包/AMEDEX 2025 PAEDIATRIC (2).pdf","source_page":200,"source_qid":null},{"record_id":"ed9adbecf8f1fc1135d4","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"AMEDEX 2025 PAEDIATRIC (2).pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX 2025.09更新包/AMEDEX 2025 PAEDIATRIC (2).pdf","source_page":201,"source_qid":null}],"parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"v27_same_page_five_structural","text_method":"tesseract_retry_200dpi_psm3","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,'needs_verification','[]','high') ON CONFLICT(id) DO UPDATE SET source_id=excluded.source_id,source_question_no=excluded.source_question_no,source_page=excluded.source_page,domain=excluded.domain,system=excluded.system,topic=excluded.topic,clinical_task=excluded.clinical_task,stem_en=excluded.stem_en,answer_key=excluded.answer_key,source_explanation_en=excluded.source_explanation_en,memory_rule_en=excluded.memory_rule_en,image_ref=excluded.image_ref,source_year=excluded.source_year,import_version=excluded.import_version,active=1,options_mode=excluded.options_mode,provenance_json=excluded.provenance_json,current_answer=excluded.current_answer,cu