not have any clinical features suggestive of any of the mentioned conditions.

Contact dermatitis (option C) is localized at and limited to the site of the contact with the culprit allergen. Moreover, systemic symptoms are not a feature.
Stevens-Johnson syndrome (option B) is a rare serious disease of the skin and mucous membranes usually as a reaction to a medication. It starts with flu-like
symptoms and is followed by a painful rash that spreads and blisters and results in sloughing off of the skin and mucous membranes. The absence of a
triggering medication and different rash characteristics make such a diagnosis unlikely.',NULL,5);
