sample obtained in the office for a more accurate assessment of the urine. With urinalysis (urine examination), the
urine sediment, as well as other signs of glomerular and/or parenchymal disease such as hematuria, red cell casts,
pyuria and/or lipiduria are assessed. Particular findings on urine examination may suggest glomerulonephritis,
nephrotic syndrome, or other renal parenchymal disorders.

Urinalysis may also suggest urinary tract infection with pyuria, bacteriuria, positive nitrites or leukocyte esterase, and
mild proteinuria.

Other initial laboratory evaluations include renal function tests (blood urea nitrogen and creatinine), serum
electrolytes, cholesterol, albumin, and total protein.

Another very important investigation to consider for this child is the urine protein/creatinine ratio on a first-morning
void obtained at home as an estimation of the amount of protein excreted in 24 hours.

(Option A) The role of renal biopsy in children with asymptomatic persistent proteinuria is controversial and is not
recommended routinely.

(Option B) If indicated, urine protein measurement is performed on a 24-hour urine collection (not 12 hours). This
test is cumbersome and not necessary unless at times if the patient is found to have an abnormal urine
protein/creatinine ratio.

(Options D and E) Other tests such as renal ultrasound, serum complement levels (C3 and C4), antinuclear
antibody (ANA), streptozyme testing, hepatitis B and C serology, and human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) testing
should be considered based on the clinical setting. A voiding cystourethrogram should be considered if there is an
abnormal ultrasound with scarring or a history of febrile urinary tract infections.',NULL,'Retain the source-era answer for question-bank study; verify current Australian guidance before clinical use.',NULL,NULL,'2025','v2.9.4',1,'text','{"source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Pediatric amedex 2025.pdf","supporting_pages":[41],"source_qid":null,"duplicate_group_id":"exact-67b5dd9fa2ca","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"ccc5d756826293e11261","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"Pediatric amedex 2025.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Pediatric amedex 2025.pdf","source_page":41,"source_qid":null},{"record_id":"65940da245003c7893d1","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"AMEDEX 2025 PAEDIATRIC (2).pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX 2025.09更新包/AMEDEX 2025 PAEDIATRIC (2).pdf","source_page":39,"source_qid":null},{"record_id":"7d75909293434e012e75","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"AMEDEX 2025 PAEDIATRIC (2).pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX 2025.09更新包/AMEDEX 2025 PAEDIATRIC (2).pdf","source_page":40,"source_qid":null},{"record_id":"2e01b0dcc9238fa55262","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"Amedex 2023.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Amedex 2023.pdf","source_page":1366,"source_qid":"1399"}],"parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"v27_same_page_five_structural","text_method":"tesseract_retry_200dpi_psm3","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,'needs_verification','[]','low') ON CONFLICT(id) DO UPDATE SET source_id=excluded.source_id,source_question_no=excluded.source_question_no,source_page=excluded.source_page,domain=excluded.domain,system=excluded.system,topic=excluded.topic,clinical_task=excluded.clinical_task,stem_en=excluded.stem_en,answer_key=excluded.answer_key,source_explanation_en=excluded.source_explanation_en,memory_rule_en=excluded.memory_rule_en,image_ref=excluded.image_ref,source_year=excluded.source_year,import_version