mmended in girls approaching puberty who have persistent VUR.\n\nNOTE -— In the past few years, the role of prophylaxis with antibiotics has been challenged. Several studies failed to find any decrease in the incidence of\nUTIs in children with VUR who take antibiotics. However, these studies had significant flaws, making it difficult to state with certainty that antibiotic\nprophylaxis is ineffective. Accordingly, the National Institutes of Health (NIH) sponsored a large prospective clinical trial of prophylaxis in an effort to\nanswer this key question. In 2014, the results of the Randomized Intervention for children with Vesicoureteral Reflux (RIVUR) study showed that antibiotic\nprophylaxis with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole was associated with a decrease of approximately 50% in the incidence of recurrent UTIs among children\nwith VUR, in comparison with placebo. Based on these results prophylactic antibiotics for all grades of VUR remain in practice.\n\nThis boy with recurrent UTIs needs continuous prophylactic antibiotics.\n(Option A) While continuous prophylactic antibiotics have been shown to decrease UTI recurrences, intermittent use of antibiotics has not proven effective.\n(Option B) Although practising good hygiene is appropriate advice, it does not specifically reduce the risk of recurrent UTIs in children with VUR.\n\n(Option C) Surgery is considered for severe VURs (grade V), girls approaching puberty whose VUR persist, or patients with recurrent UTIs despite\nprophylaxis. A grade II reflux is very likely to resolve spontaneously with growth and only needs medical management.\n\n(Option E) Circumcision of infant males with VUR may be considered based on an increased risk of urinary tract infections in boys who are not circumcised\ncompared to those who are. Although there is insufficient data to evaluate the degree of this increased risk and its duration, parents need to be made aware\nof this association so that they can decide whether their children are circumcised.","source_answer_status":"present","current_answer":null,"current_answer_status":"needs_verification","current_evidence":[],"currentness_risk":"high","currentness_keywords":["age","antibiotic","dose","duration","management","prophyl","treatment"],"likely_visual_question":false,"parser_confidence":"medium","parser_mode":"v27_same_page_five_structural","text_method":"tesseract_retry_200dpi_psm3","parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","stem_hash":"b1f33482ac762d74c354a8009842e9d3d977a169","duplicate_group_id":"exact-b1f33482ac76","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"e5054b2abeab6c3557f7","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"Pediatric amedex 2025.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Pediatric amedex 2025.pdf","source_page":62,"source_qid":null}]}',1,'v2.9.4','2026-09-24T00:00:00.000Z') ON CONFLICT(record_id) DO UPDATE SET source_file=excluded.source_file,source_page=excluded.source_page,domain=excluded.domain,stem=excluded.stem,options_json=excluded.options_json,source_answer=excluded.source_answer,source_explanation=excluded.source_explanation,provenance_json=excluded.provenance_json,visual_asset=excluded.visual_asset,visual_url=excluded.visual_url,options_mode=excluded.options_mode,current_answer=excluded.current_answer,current_answer_status=excluded.current_answer_status,current_evidence_json=excluded.current_evidence_json,currentness_risk=excluded.currentness_risk,record_json=excluded.record_json,active_quiz=excluded.active_quiz,import_version=excluded.import_version,imported_at=excluded.imported_at;
